  <!-- 3. DELTA -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The Israeli coalition dissolution process advanced materially on May 20 when 110 of 120 MKs voted in favor of a preliminary reading of a dissolution bill — 110-0, with Netanyahu absent from the chamber. The bill was submitted not by the opposition but by coalition whip Ofir Katz, making this a managed parliamentary exit rather than an adversarial ouster: Netanyahu's bloc chose to control the dissolution timeline rather than absorb a formal no-confidence defeat. Three additional plenum readings plus a House Committee stage are required before formal dissolution; no election date has been set. The resolution criteria for <span class="cite">F2</span> (coalition collapse before Oct 27) have not been triggered — no Knesset roll-call has confirmed majority loss on a confidence or budget vote — and the resolution criteria for <span class="cite">F5</span> (formal early election announcement by June 1) have also not been triggered, as a preliminary reading does not constitute final dissolution. The functional coalition seat count stands at approximately 60, one below the 61-seat threshold, with Smotrich's Religious Zionism party not having formally departed. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>The probability that Netanyahu formally names an election date before June 1 now stands at 0.40, moved from 0.22. The 110-0 preliminary vote is the strongest directional reading yet on the dissolution path, and the coalition's own authorship of the bill removes the earlier uncertainty about whether opposition pressure alone could drive the timeline. Three readings in eleven days is procedurally feasible but not routine; the House Committee has not publicly committed to a compressed schedule. A Netanyahu-Haredi deal restoring a 61-seat majority remains the primary scenario in which the bill is shelved, and no such deal has been announced. The new <span class="cite">F6</span> forecast (Knesset dissolution bill passing third reading by June 30) carries a 40-day horizon and a probability of 0.65, reflecting the longer runway available for the remaining procedural steps. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>On the Iran-US diplomacy track, Iran's May 18 counter-proposal — transmitted via Pakistan — demanded reparations for war damage, a full US troop withdrawal from the region, an end to the naval blockade, sanctions removal, and frozen asset release, with no nuclear concessions offered. Trump described an earlier Iranian proposal as "totally unacceptable." As of May 20, Trump told reporters the two sides are "right on the borderline" between restarting hostilities and a deal, and gave "a few days" for a resolution. A Qatar-Pakistan revised bridging memo is reportedly in circulation, and Pakistan's interior minister made a second Tehran visit within the week. The specific enrichment duration gap — Tehran's public floor at 5 years, Washington's demand at 20 years or HEU transfer — has not narrowed in any public statement from either principal. No Witkoff-Araghchi direct meeting has been confirmed in the sourced record since May 15. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>The probability of a signed framework by June 5 is 0.20, reduced from 0.25. The new <span class="cite">F8</span> forecast (enrichment gap bridged by June 5) carries a separate probability of 0.15, reflecting that the specific numeric alignment required by that resolution criterion is a more demanding threshold than a framework signing. The <span class="cite">F7</span> forecast (Iran-US ceasefire holds through June 17) is set at 0.62: the ceasefire has held without a confirmed US or Israeli strike on Iranian territory throughout the period, and a diplomatic breakdown — while possible — does not automatically trigger resumed strikes on Iranian territory given the acknowledged costs both governments have expressed publicly. → supports <span class="cite">F7</span> without move</p>

      <p>Hormuz commercial transit data through May 20-21 is structurally unchanged. Approximately 2 commercial vessels per day are transiting — against the 50-vessel resolution threshold — with shadow-fleet traffic constituting roughly 80% of what does move. Lloyd's war-risk premiums stand at 8–10% of hull value, against the 1.5% indicator threshold; at current premium levels, a single Hormuz voyage costs $10–14 million in insurance alone. Hapag-Lloyd publicly repeated on May 20 that Hormuz transit is "not yet possible." The IRGC announced a "coordinated passage" toll system on May 20; under the forecast's ambiguity rule, such IRGC-managed transits would not count toward the 50/day threshold and would qualify for AMBIGUOUS-RESOLVE-AS-NO treatment. Project Freedom was paused by Trump on May 6 and has not been restarted. The <span class="cite">F1</span> probability is reduced to 0.15 from 0.38. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>The Lebanon ceasefire forecast (<span class="cite">F4</span>) reached its May 22 horizon date without meeting any resolution criteria. The US State Department formally announced a 45-day extension on May 15-16, reaffirmed by spokesman Tommy Pigott; the Lebanese government welcomed the extension. No Beirut-governorate IDF airstrike was detected in the May 19-21 window. No Hezbollah barrage above 50 rockets per day was recorded. IDF Chief Zamir's "no ceasefire" framing describes an operational posture in southern Lebanon — where IDF strikes killed 10 civilians in Deir Qanun al-Nahr, Tyre district — not a formal government withdrawal from the diplomatic arrangement. Pentagon security talks are scheduled for May 29; political talks are set for June 2-3. <span class="cite">F4</span> resolves NO. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

