      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (23%):</strong> Iran's IRGC threat language and Trump's "few days" pressure sustain the kinetics tail, but the ceasefire has held without an Iran-territory strike since the pause and escalation requires both diplomatic collapse and Trump ordering resumed strikes on Iranian territory.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (59%):</strong> Neither a signed framework (F3, 20%) nor a Hormuz reopening (F1, 15%) is probable within their horizons; Israel's dissolution path (F5, F6) changes the Israeli domestic landscape without altering the Iran-US military equilibrium, leaving the conflict in its current form: ceasefire on Iranian territory, active proxy combat across Lebanon and Yemen, nuclear talks stalled.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (18%):</strong> The primary path is F3 (signed deal by June 5, 20%); a signed framework triggering sanctions relief and Hormuz reopening would constitute de-escalation; F8 (enrichment gap bridged, 15%) is a necessary precondition, but the 15-year enrichment duration gap and Iran's reparations demand leave this path narrow.</p>
    </div>
