  <!-- 5. INSIDE IRAN -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media through the May 19-21 window maintained a dual-track public posture: sovereignty framing on one channel, conditional diplomatic engagement on the other. IRNA's military-sourced reporting featured General Mohammad Akraminia warning of "surprising options" if adversaries made another "miscalculation," with the threat characterized as extending to areas "the enemy has not anticipated." Tasnim — the IRGC-linked semi-official agency — was the primary outlet for the content of Iran's May 18 counter-proposal: reparations, US troop withdrawal, blockade termination, sanctions removal, frozen asset release, with the nuclear program characterized as non-negotiable at this stage. This framing is consistent with Araghchi's BRICS statement ("no trust") and Deputy FM Gharibabadi's repeated public formulation that Iran is protecting "legitimate rights," not offering "concessions." → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>
      <p>The internal factional division documented by Iran International — between Raja News (Jalili-aligned hardliners opposing any deal) and Tasnim (Ghalibaf-aligned pragmatists more open to structured engagement) — continued through this period. Pezeshkian's "we will never bow" statement circulated without contradiction in state media. IRNA's coverage of Pakistan's May 20 mediation visit framed Tehran as the constructive party "bridging gaps," keeping substantive nuclear concessions off the public record while claiming diplomatic initiative. No Iranian state media outlet acknowledged any US nuclear demand as acceptable; the framing of enrichment as a sovereign right remained consistent across IRIB, IRNA, and Tasnim. The enrichment duration gap — 5 years versus 20 years — is presented as a red line with Khamenei-level endorsement, which constrains what any Iranian negotiating team could publicly accept within the <span class="cite">F3</span> and <span class="cite">F8</span> horizons. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

